      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "54169790-2ab8-5f39-a1a5-95cf2c7b7d2c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Jackson Davis, Francis Key Hunt, Francis Scott Key\n- Places: Loudoun House, Terra Rubra, Frederick, Maryland, Georgetown, D.C., Washington, D.C.\n- Roles: architect, lawyer, author, amateur poet\n- Works: \"The Defence of Fort McHenry\", \"The Star-Spangled Banner\"\n\nFacts:\n- Francis Scott Key was born in 1779 at Terra Rubra.\n- Francis Scott Key was an American lawyer, author, and amateur poet from Frederick, Maryland, and later Georgetown, D.C., near Washington, D.C.\n- Francis Scott Key wrote the lyrics to a poem first titled \"The Defence of Fort McHenry,\" which later became the United States national anthem, \"The Star-Spangled Banner,\" when set to an old English gentlemen's society tune.\n- Loudoun House was designed by New York architect Alexander Jackson Davis and built in 1851 for Francis Key Hunt.\n- Francis Key Hunt lived from 1817 to 1879 and was named after his mother's cousin, Francis Scott Key.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5270e9a2-5866-53df-8a10-e1ad25c7cf28",
        "text": "Page: Loudoun House. Sentence: Designed by New York architect Alexander Jackson Davis, the house was built in 1851 for Francis Key Hunt (1817–1879), who was named after his mother's cousin, Francis Scott Key.\nPage: Francis Scott Key. Sentence: Francis Scott Key (August 1, 1779January 11, 1843) was an American lawyer, author, and amateur poet from Frederick, Maryland and later Georgetown, D.C., near Washington, D.C. who wrote the lyrics for a poem entitled at first \"The Defence of Fort McHenry\", which when set to an old English gentlemens' society tune, eventually became the United States' national anthem, \"The Star-Spangled Banner\".\nPage: Terra Rubra. Sentence: It was the birth site of Francis Scott Key in 1779.",
